canonical_claim.statement:
As printed (p. 347): ‘Freedman’s (2008) results straightforwardly imply that a Wald-type test using $\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})$ is an asymptotically valid test of Fisher’s null, but that it may not be a valid test of the null that $\tau=0$ when treatment effects are heterogeneous (as is possible under Neyman’s null).’ As printed (p. 347): ‘Via standard linearization arguments, $\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})$ will provide a better approximation to $V(\widehat{\tau})$ in large samples when effects are indeed nonlocal and constant.’ As printed (p. 347): ‘Under Pitman-type local alternatives of the form $\tau_N=o(1)$, we have $\widehat{V}(\text{Fisher})-\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})=o_p(N^{-1})$. The only asymptotic distinction between $\widehat{V}(\text{Fisher})$ and $\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})$ emerges with nonlocal alternatives.’ As printed (p. 346, Introduction, item (i)): ‘Rather than the FRT, a Wald-type analogue to the FRT using the difference in means exists (considered by Freedman, 2008, Samii and Aronow, 2012, Gerber and Green, 2012, and Lin, 2013) is a valid test of Fisher’s null, and does not suffer from the potential pathology of Rao-type tests. Furthermore, this Wald-type test—equivalent to a pooled-variance two-sample $z$-test—is asymptotically equivalent to the FRT under local alternatives.’

Formal reading (the audit's; S4 and S5). Along every sequence of finite populations that satisfies S5:
(a) [local alternatives] if in addition $\tau_N\to0$, then $N\left[\widehat{V}(\text{Fisher})-\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})\right]\to0$ in probability; if moreover $\frac{\sigma_1^2}{1-p}+\frac{\sigma_0^2}{p}>0$, then $\widehat{V}(\text{Fisher})/\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})\to1$ in probability;
(b) [constant effects, of which Fisher's null is the case $\tau_N=0$] if $Y_i(1)-Y_i(0)=\tau_N$ for every $i$ and every $N$, then $N\left[\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})-V(\widehat{\tau})\right]\to0$ in probability and $N\left[\widehat{V}(\text{Fisher})-V(\widehat{\tau})\right]-\tau_N^2\to0$ in probability;
(c) [asymptotic validity for Fisher's null] if $Y_i(1)=Y_i(0)$ for every $i$ and every $N$, and $\sigma_0^2>0$, then $\widehat{\tau}/\sqrt{\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})}$ converges in distribution to the standard normal law; hence, for $0<\alpha<1$, the test that rejects when $|\widehat{\tau}|/\sqrt{\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})}>z_{1-\alpha/2}$ (the standard normal quantile) has rejection probability tending to $\alpha$.
Part (c) rests on the finite-population central limit theorem, which the comment invokes on p. 347 (‘given the finite population central limit theorem’). The clause ‘it may not be a valid test of the null that $\tau=0$ when treatment effects are heterogeneous’ is part (c) of target P053-T8.

As printed (pp. 347–348): ‘Applying arguments from Neyman (1990), it is well known that $\lim_{N\to\infty}NV(\widehat{\tau})\le S_1^2/p+S_0^2/(1-p)$, where $p=\lim_{N\to\infty}N_1/N$. Ergo, given the finite population central limit theorem, Wald-type tests constructed using $\widehat{V}(\text{Neyman})$ will tend to be conservative. Under constant effects, Neyman (1990) further shows that this holds with equality: $\lim_{N\to\infty}NV(\widehat{\tau})=S_1^2/p+S_0^2/(1-p)$. Furthermore, if the maintained hypothesis of constant effects is true, then it must be the case that $S_1=S_0$ and, therefore, it would also be the case that $\lim_{N\to\infty}NV(\widehat{\tau})=S_0^2/p+S_1^2/(1-p)$; in such a case, $\widehat{V}(\text{Neyman})$ and $\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})$ therefore asymptotically agree. If we are not in the world of constant effects, it is possible that $S_0\neq S_1$, and there exist limit distributions such that $\lim_{N\to\infty}NV(\widehat{\tau})>S_0^2/p+S_1^2/(1-p)$, implying that using $\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})$ may yield anticonservative inferences and invalid tests of Neyman’s null.’

Formal reading (the audit's; S4 and S5). Along every sequence of finite populations that satisfies S5:
(a) [Neyman's bound] $\lim_{N\to\infty}NV(\widehat{\tau})=\frac{\sigma_1^2}{p}+\frac{\sigma_0^2}{1-p}-\sigma_\tau^2\le\frac{\sigma_1^2}{p}+\frac{\sigma_0^2}{1-p}$, the right side being the probability limit of $N\widehat{V}(\text{Neyman})$;
(b) [constant effects] if $Y_i(1)-Y_i(0)=\tau_N$ for every $i$ and every $N$, then $S_1=S_0$ and $S_\tau=0$ for every $N$, $\lim_{N\to\infty}NV(\widehat{\tau})=\frac{\sigma_1^2}{p}+\frac{\sigma_0^2}{1-p}=\frac{\sigma_0^2}{p}+\frac{\sigma_1^2}{1-p}$, and $N\left[\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})-\widehat{V}(\text{Neyman})\right]\to0$ in probability;
(c) [existence] there is a sequence of finite populations that satisfies S5 with $\lim_{N\to\infty}NV(\widehat{\tau})>\frac{\sigma_0^2}{p}+\frac{\sigma_1^2}{1-p}$, the right side being the probability limit of $N\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})$. Witness (the Sol reader's): for $m\ge1$ let $N=10m$, $N_1=2m$, $N_0=8m$, $Y_i(0)=1$ for $5m$ units and $Y_i(0)=-1$ for the other $5m$, and $Y_i(1)=2Y_i(0)$ for every $i$. Then $\tau=0$ (Neyman's null holds and the effects are heterogeneous), $S_0^2=S_\tau^2=\frac{N}{N-1}$, $S_1^2=\frac{4N}{N-1}$, and for every $m$: $V(\widehat{\tau})=\frac{81}{4(N-1)}$, $\frac{S_1^2}{N_0}+\frac{S_0^2}{N_1}=\frac{10}{N-1}$ and $\frac{S_1^2}{N_1}+\frac{S_0^2}{N_0}=\frac{85}{4(N-1)}$. So $p=\frac15$, $\sigma_1^2=4$, $\sigma_0^2=\sigma_\tau^2=1$, and $\lim NV(\widehat{\tau})=\frac{81}{4}$ lies above $10=\frac{\sigma_0^2}{p}+\frac{\sigma_1^2}{1-p}$ and below $\frac{85}{4}=\frac{\sigma_1^2}{p}+\frac{\sigma_0^2}{1-p}$.
The words ‘will tend to be conservative’, ‘anticonservative inferences’ and ‘invalid tests of Neyman’s null’ are consequences drawn through the finite-population central limit theorem; the target is the variance statements.

As printed (pp. 346–347, Section 2): ‘Ding (2017) further reproduces Lin (2013) and Samii and Aronow’s (2012) result, showing that a Wald test from a linear model allowing for heteroskedasticity is equivalent to a Wald-type test using one of Neyman’s variance estimators, $$\widehat{V}(\text{Neyman})=S_1^2/N_1+S_0^2/N_0+o_p\left(N^{-1}\right).$$’ As printed (p. 347, Section 3): ‘Ding [(2017), equation (7)], considers $\widehat{V}(\text{Fisher})-\widehat{V}(\text{Neyman})$; let us consider an analogue, replacing $\widehat{V}(\text{Fisher})$ with its Wald-type analogue $\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})$: $$\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})-\widehat{V}(\text{Neyman})=\left(S_1^2/N_0+S_0^2/N_1\right)-\left(S_1^2/N_1+S_0^2/N_0\right)+o_p\left(N^{-1}\right).$$ A necessary condition for $\operatorname{plim}_{N\to\infty}N\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})\neq\operatorname{plim}_{N\to\infty}N\widehat{V}(\text{Neyman})$ is $S_1\neq S_0$. [As noted above, this result also holds for $\widehat{V}(\text{Fisher})-\widehat{V}(\text{Neyman})$ under any Pitman-type alternative such that $\tau_N=o(1)$.] We unpack this result.’

Formal reading (the audit's; S4 and S5). Along every sequence of finite populations that satisfies S5:
(a) $N\left[\widehat{V}(\text{Neyman})-\left(\frac{S_1^2}{N_1}+\frac{S_0^2}{N_0}\right)\right]\to0$ in probability;
(b) $N\left[\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})-\widehat{V}(\text{Neyman})-\left(\left(\frac{S_1^2}{N_0}+\frac{S_0^2}{N_1}\right)-\left(\frac{S_1^2}{N_1}+\frac{S_0^2}{N_0}\right)\right)\right]\to0$ in probability;
(c) $N\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})\to\frac{\sigma_1^2}{1-p}+\frac{\sigma_0^2}{p}$ and $N\widehat{V}(\text{Neyman})\to\frac{\sigma_1^2}{p}+\frac{\sigma_0^2}{1-p}$ in probability, and the difference of the two limits is $\frac{(\sigma_1^2-\sigma_0^2)(2p-1)}{p(1-p)}$; hence, if the two probability limits differ, then $\sigma_1\neq\sigma_0$ (and $p\neq\frac12$);
(d) if in addition $\tau_N\to0$, then (b) and (c) hold with $\widehat{V}(\text{Fisher})$ in place of $\widehat{V}(\text{OLS})$.
